<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A phone-charging kiosk is to be the same distance from school <m>A({-1}, {-1})</m> as from school <m>B({9}, {-1})</m>, and exactly <m>{10}</m> km from the market <m>C({10}, {-2})</m>. Grid units are kilometres.</p><p>(a) The locus of points equidistant from <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> is a line.</p><div><m>{x} = </m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) The kiosk can be at two places on this line. Give their <m>{y}</m>-coordinates.</p><div><fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/> and <fillin characters="5" name="ans3"/>   </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> have the same <m>{y}</m>-coordinate, so <m>AB</m> is horizontal. Its perpendicular bisector passes through the midpoint <m>({4}, {-1})</m> at right angles to <m>AB</m>: <m>{x} = {4}</m>.</p><p>(b) <m>C</m> is <m>{6}</m> km from the line <m>{x} = {4}</m>. A point on the line <m>{10}</m> km from <m>C</m> is <m>\sqrt{ {10}^2 - {6}^2 } = {8}</m> km along the line from the point nearest <m>C</m>, on either side: <m>{y} = {-2} - {8} = {-10}</m> and <m>{y} = {-2} + {8} = {6}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>4</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>-10</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>6</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>